# Run when labwc starts.
#
# UPSTREAM SHIPS AN autostart EXAMPLE TO share/doc/labwc/ AND INSTALLS NOTHING.
# Without this file the session starts with a bare compositor: no wallpaper, no
# status bar, nothing but whatever the user launches by hand.
#
# EACH LINE IS GUARDED because this file runs on every start and a missing
# binary would print a shell error into the session log on every boot. The
# guard makes an absent package silent rather than noisy -- and the packages
# ARE optional: labwc runs without any of them.

# A background. swaybg reads PNG ONLY here: gdk-pixbuf is declined in its
# recipe, so background-image.c:41 goes straight to
# cairo_image_surface_create_from_png and any other format fails to load.
#
# THE IMAGE IS A GRADIENT WITH A CENTRED MARKER, NOT A FLAT COLOUR, AND THAT IS
# FOR THE SCREENSHOT. A screenshot of a solid field looks identical whether the
# compositor rendered it or the framebuffer was never written at all. A
# gradient proves per-pixel output; the marker block proves geometry and
# orientation. -c is kept as the fallback so a missing file still gives a
# desktop rather than a black screen.
if [ -x /usr/bin/swaybg ]; then
    if [ -f /usr/share/veron/wallpaper.png ]; then
        /usr/bin/swaybg -m fill -i /usr/share/veron/wallpaper.png &
    else
        /usr/bin/swaybg -c '#1a1a1a' &
    fi
fi

# The status bar. THE config.yml GUARD USED TO BE TRUE OF A FILE THAT DID NOT
# EXIST, so yambar was built with ten plugins enabled and never started, and
# the guard is what made that silent. The file is now shipped beside this one.
#
# Its foreign-toplevel module is what shows the window list: labwc implements
# wlr-foreign-toplevel-management through wlroots, so opening a terminal makes
# an entry appear in the bar. That is the cheapest end-to-end proof that
# compositor, protocol and bar are all talking.
#
# -c IS REQUIRED: yambar DOES NOT SEARCH XDG_CONFIG_DIRS. main.c's
# get_config_path checks $HOME/.config/yambar/config.yml and then
# $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/yambar/config.yml, and stops. /etc/xdg is in
# XDG_CONFIG_DIRS, which it never consults -- so the file shipped beside this
# one was invisible and yambar died with
#
#     err: main.c:316: could not find a configuration (see man 5 yambar)
#
# labwc reads /etc/xdg through XDG_CONFIG_DIRS and yambar does not; assuming
# one spec-adjacent behaviour implies the other is what cost this. -c names
# the path outright (main.c:220, which stats it before accepting).
[ -x /usr/bin/yambar ] && [ -f /etc/xdg/yambar/config.yml ] && \
    /usr/bin/yambar -c /etc/xdg/yambar/config.yml &
